/*****************************************************************************
    jitJRt4 -- F4. A MID-BLOCK RETURN, WITH STATEMENTS AFTER IT. 2026-08-05.

    ⚠ E1, AND IT IS THE EDGE MOST LIKELY TO PRODUCE VALID-BUT-WRONG IR. A return
    above the body's end TERMINATES the current block, and LLVM forbids code
    after a terminator -- so the walk that keeps going has to emit somewhere
    legal. jitEmitReturn parks the builder in a fresh unreachable block
    (`afterReturn`), which is jitEmitContinue's existing idiom lifted rather than
    reinvented; the optimiser drops it.

    ⚠ AND THE WALK **MUST** KEEP GOING, which is the counter-intuitive half.
    aCTionBlocK carries `if jitting continue;` because at EMIT time the
    statements after a branch are REACHABLE TEXT -- they run whenever the branch
    is not taken -- even though at RUN time the branch means stop. One flag, two
    eras. If that were not there, `mbTail = 999` and the second return would
    simply not exist in the IR and fire 2 would be wrong.

    WHAT IS ASSERTED, and both fires exercise the OPPOSITE arm:
      fire 1  mbIn = 7  -> the early return is TAKEN.     returns 111,
              and mbTail stays 0 -- THE TAIL DID NOT EXECUTE.
      fire 2  mbIn = 3  -> the early return is NOT taken. returns 222,
              and mbTail is 999 -- the tail DID execute.
    111 and 222 are different answers, not merely different inputs. And mbTail
    is the second channel: a returned value alone cannot distinguish "the tail
    was skipped" from "the tail ran and its value was discarded".

    ⚠ mbTail IS INITIALISED IN THE BODY, not left to a default. A row expecting
    0 that a never-written field would also produce asserts nothing; setting it
    to 0 at the top of the body makes 0 a value the action WROTE.

    SENTINEL AT THE FOOT, exactly ONE stop() (rule H2).
*****************************************************************************/
Start();
include(unitTests);
include(generate);
include(utilities);
search reset stack Grokking;
search Generating UnitTests bcOPs Utilities list;
register(JitLadder);
define
    mbIn;
    mbTail;
    mbF code={
        mbTail = 0;
        if mbIn > 5;
            return 111;
        mbTail = 999;
        return 222;
        };
    ;
/*  ⚠ mbTail IS GIVEN A DATA TYPE HERE, AND IT HAS TO BE. A field that has never
    been assigned has no datA type, so it prints as its own TAG -- and the jitted
    store writes its baked/frame slot without stamping one, so the row reads
    `mbTail` instead of a number and says nothing either way. -1 rather than 0,
    so neither expected value (0 for fire 1, 999 for fire 2) can be produced by
    the initialisation itself. Same trap as incant/jitSelfFn's sfOut.  */
mbTail = -1;

mbIn = 7;
print "JRt4 fire 1 setup : mbIn =" mbIn " (early return TAKEN)":;
testing(mbF);
print "JRt4 fire 1 tail  : mbTail =" mbTail " (want 0 -- the tail did NOT run)":;

mbIn = 3;
print "JRt4 fire 2 setup : mbIn =" mbIn " (early return NOT taken, recompile: NONE)":;
jitRefire();
print "JRt4 fire 2 tail  : mbTail =" mbTail " (want 999 -- the tail DID run)":;

print "JRt4 SENTINEL":;
stop();
